\section{The trap this design avoids}{

Do \strong{not} use the alias to assign an EIN, and do not block on it. If the
alias merely surfaces the BMF row for \verb{Nourishing Hope} as a candidate for the
query \verb{Lakeview Pantry}, the comparison step scores those two names against
each other, gets ~0, and the veto layer discards the correct answer. The match
is justified by the alias \emph{observation}, not by name similarity. So the alias
name is written into the reference as a row in its own right, and the existing
\code{exact-name} pass resolves it with no new comparison logic.
